the Q&amp;A document that will be linked to our
webinar and courses page and on the FTP site,

00:00:19.099 --> 00:00:21.010
in the materials directory.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:24.590
That is given to you as a compressed URL.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:30.600
I am going to turn this over to Rana Morris
and she will talk to about APIs.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.100
Thank you for joining us today.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.910
Today is a little different than our usual
NCBI Minutes.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:39.350
It will be a little longer.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:45.000
We will talk about APIs developed at NCBI.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.230
This is an introduction to NCBI APIs for people
who are new to them.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:54.840
And for those who are API savvy just to give
you an I an idea of the diversity and breadth

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.850
of APIs available at NCBI.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:05.659
For those of you who are using APIs such as
the e-utilities you may not learn too much

00:01:05.659 --> 00:01:11.479
in this talk but we do want to hear from you
and we are interested in if you are wanting

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:12.670
to learn more.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:16.860
If you have a specific question about how
to use a particular API or problems with the

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:21.810
script, please send a message into the helpdesk
(info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov) and we will work directly

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.270
with you to help you with your script.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:28.320
After this webinar, we will send a survey
to ask everyone for feedback.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.150
We are interested in the specific API topics
you want to learn more about.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:41.640
And also what functionality or access to specific
data at NCBI you want to have added to our

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.260
existing repertoire of APIs.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.010
If you have other ideas we would like to hear
from you.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.740
For beginners, what is an API?

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.869
What can it do for you?

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:55.330
Just to give a quick set of definitions.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.890
URLs which you know about our web addresses.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.250
They are composed of two main components.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:09.060
A protocol identifier like HTTP or HTTPS,
FTP, SFTP, and a resource name.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.430
Such as the ncbi URL or blast URL.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:16.439
Those will get you direct links to the servers.

00:02:16.439 --> 00:02:22.739
APIs are URLs with specific syntaxes that
have been designed for developers to use within

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:28.230
their scripts or applications or programs,
to interact with servers across the Internet.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:35.650
NCBI APIs have been developed to access, process,
and retrieve data from our backend servers.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.459
We are also using our own services now.

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:44.030
PubChem as an example we are using our own
APIs but there are other commercial companies

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:50.299
and other people who are using APIs, including
EndNote, BioGPS, there is a Molecules app

00:02:50.299 --> 00:02:52.040
for the iPad and more.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:57.349
I am going to describe APIs for beginners.

00:02:57.349 --> 00:03:00.239
As kind of an action based sentence with specific
grammatical parts.

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:06.090
I will show you how to do this in a minute
with our most popular API that so many people

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.090
are using.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:13.689
What I will cover today are really four specific
APIs that we have.

00:03:13.689 --> 00:03:15.059
There's a whole bunch more.

00:03:15.059 --> 00:03:19.269
I want to show you where to find the information
on these and then I will show you where you

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:22.519
can keep up and find out more information
in general.

00:03:22.519 --> 00:03:28.610
We will talk about EDirect which is really
not an API but uses APIs and it may be an

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:34.169
alternative if you don't want to write your
own scripts but you want to be able to process

00:03:34.169 --> 00:03:37.489
information from our backend servers.

00:03:37.489 --> 00:03:41.939
Our most popular API is virtually sitewide.

00:03:41.939 --> 00:03:47.400
Almost all of our databases, our Entrez databases,
and it's called the Entrez Programming Utilities.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.400
Sometimes called the EUtilities or EUtils.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:58.120
This is a set of structured URL calls to access
our databases and I will show you examples

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:01.669
as to how to use these.

00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:08.359
There are about 25 million EUtils calls that
are actually sent to our servers every day

00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:11.900
from about 100,000 different IP addresses.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:17.389
This is a little more than is hitting from
our front end servers.

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:19.310
We have a lot more people who use those.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:25.600
About 4.2 million people use the web services
every day that are making these calls.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.470
This is a popular resource.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:33.940
Different types of things you can do and these
are a small set of examples.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.430
You can search PubMed with the term and retrieve
the PubMed identifiers.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:44.840
You can search MedGen with a SnoMedCT and
then link over to GTR for summary information

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:49.440
for the genetic tests related to that ID.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:55.750
You can post a list of GeneID's and fetch
there full records, even post a PubChem compound

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:59.500
CID and see what the molecule looks like.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:06.150
We have a lot of information about the EUtils
. I will show you a book and a bigger slide

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:10.700
to show you how to get the information to
use these things.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:16.220
There are two YouTube videos on our YouTube
site one is only four minutes long it's a

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:17.520
quick introduction.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:18.540
One is a full hour.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.350
That will give you a lot of detail.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.900
Those are the links there.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.780
The key thing about the EUtils is the base
URL.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:35.500
Any time you see eutils in the URL you will
notice that happens to be calling our EUtils

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.530
API.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:43.980
This is the book of instructions to help you
figure out how to use the EUtilities.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.740
As a matter of fact you have a table of contents
on the right-hand side this is the bookshelf.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:54.070
There's an introduction and it has a whole
description of all the different EUtilities,

00:05:54.070 --> 00:06:00.900
and even some sample applications and scripts
to show you as examples how to use them.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:07.330
The key to using any API is not only to know
which server to hit, but also where to operate.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:12.950
In our case we think of it as a noun in a
sentence as where to operate and what kind

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:16.030
of database or what kind of data you want
to get.

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:21.700
This is an example from our gquery page that
shows you all the different databases that

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:23.470
we have at NCBI.

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:30.520
It is a wide diversity of different types
of data.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:35.780
And EUtilities works pretty much in all of
the Entrez databases.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:40.000
Here is the base URL with your EUtils.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:46.920
That's what you want to use for every call
for an API involving the EUtilities.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.520
You need to add a couple of things to your
sentences.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.500
Need to have verbs that tell you what you
want to do and then nouns.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:07:01.890
So as an example, in that red box you can
put a verb which - - these are examples of

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:06.700
some of the calls you can make to tell the
EUtilities what to do.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:12.870
You can do a search you could post you could
or create a summary or grab a fetch a record

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.830
or link between databases.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:19.120
Then you need a noun to tell you what are
you starting off with?

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:23.680
I have a text query I want to perform in esearch
with.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:30.710
I have a set of IDs that I want to post or
grab the esummaries from or I want to fetch

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:36.400
the whole record or i want to link from one
set of IDs to a database and get something

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.530
back on the other end.

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:40.820
These are the things that you get on the other
end.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:44.210
One of these particular verbs.

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:45.290
Here's an example.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:49.080
Let's say you want to get a summary of information
about a gene.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:52.770
You know the Gene ID which happens to be 3269.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:57.840
That happens to be the ID for the human histamine
receptor H1.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:00.650
Here's how to structure one of the EUtilities
APIs.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:06.310
You have your base URL call which will identify
the server you want to go to , eutils.

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:08.250
You have a verb.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:13.380
In this case an essummary, get me the E summary
for this particular record.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:16.430
I want to go in this particular database.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:19.310
I want to use this particular term.

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.880
You can also specify what the format is.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:23.550
Right now we will go with the default.

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:27.419
We will show you what that is in a moment.

00:08:27.419 --> 00:08:32.860
It's hard to see, but this is, if you copy
paste the URL and put it into a web browser,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:40.159
you get back a structured list here in XML
which happens to be the default that shows

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:47.399
you what you get back which is the esummary
in XML format for the histamine H1 from the

00:08:47.399 --> 00:08:49.560
Gene database.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:56.350
Another example could be trying to find PubMed
record IDs for this Gene ID.

00:08:56.350 --> 00:09:03.529
In the Gene database we are able to link directly
from the gene to records in PubMed that have

00:09:03.529 --> 00:09:07.910
been identified as referring to this particular
gene.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:13.230
I'm only showing you the first half, the back
half of the API call.

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:18.230
You would still use the same base call all
the way up here through eutils in blue, and

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:24.720
instead of esummary you would use elink and
you would want to link from one database,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:27.740
Gene, to another one, PubMed.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:32.540
We will start off with our ID again 3269.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:36.350
This is what it looks like.

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:39.240
Again it's in XML which is the standard format.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:42.070
We can change the format and we will show
you that.

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:51.180
We actually - - starting off from Gene to
the DB PubMed, here's your id, 3269, and then

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:58.749
these are different PubMed identifiers that
correspond to that gene record.

00:09:58.749 --> 00:10:02.449
I showed you things in XML and I showed you
e-summaries.

00:10:02.449 --> 00:10:05.660
We can change the format of how we get things
back.

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:08.930
Maybe don't like XML you want a different
format.

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:15.319
You can do that in the utilities by using
the retype parameter and specify what you

00:10:15.319 --> 00:10:19.009
want to return it as.

00:10:19.009 --> 00:10:22.149
So this is what it looks like.

00:10:22.149 --> 00:10:31.589
Here as a e-summary from PubMed this happens
to be one of the IDs we saw before.

00:10:31.589 --> 00:10:38.319
You will see over here in the screen below,
this long XML with all the authors and the

00:10:38.319 --> 00:10:44.970
title and the information that is in the summary
about that particular ID.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:50.680
If you add retype at the end, in orange here,
and you say medline you will get it back in

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:51.829
that format.

00:10:51.829 --> 00:10:56.870
You librarians who happen to be on the line
might be very familiar with that.

00:10:56.870 --> 00:11:02.899
Or in this particular case we are doing and
E fetch and that's getting the entire record

00:11:02.899 --> 00:11:04.790
which includes the abstract.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:09.149
You won't have that in your summary but you
can get that back in XML.

00:11:09.149 --> 00:11:13.110
You can specify it or if you don't put the
Rettype and you will automatically get that

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:16.529
back in XML.

00:11:16.529 --> 00:11:24.449
These EUtils call are useful not just individually
but useful for eutils pipelines.

00:11:24.449 --> 00:11:30.570
You can start off with one eutils call and
daisy chain it to another and another to do

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:31.570
something useful.

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:33.850
Here is an example of what you can do.

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:38.800
Let's say you start off with the disease hereditary
hemochromatosis which happens to have a specific

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:40.740
Snomed CT ID.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:45.540
You want to do a search in our disease database
MedGen.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:49.600
We will do an esearch in MedGen.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:54.300
That will get us back a set of MedGen id's.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:12:01.399
Let's say you want to find not just the MedGen
ID's but you want to find it in the genetic

00:12:01.399 --> 00:12:07.850
testing registry to find out if there are
tests for hereditary hemochromatosis.

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:17.949
What you can do then is actually take those
MedGen IDs and use elink to go from MedGen

00:12:17.949 --> 00:12:20.779
into GTR or the genetic testing registry.

00:12:20.779 --> 00:12:26.389
That will get you back a bunch of GTR or genetic
testing registry identifiers.

00:12:26.389 --> 00:12:30.631
And then, the id's are nice but you'd like
to get human readable information so that

00:12:30.631 --> 00:12:37.559
you can pull the esummary of the GTR IDs and
then in the summary information you will be

00:12:37.559 --> 00:12:41.079
able to get who is giving the test and who
to contact.

00:12:41.079 --> 00:12:48.920
So you can make three EUtilities calls and
start out with one input and get something

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:51.639
hopefully very useful out of it.

00:12:51.639 --> 00:12:57.249
The book on the bookshelf for the Entrez programming
utilities has lots of examples.

00:12:57.249 --> 00:13:00.749
They have a lot of examples of these EUtilities
calls.

00:13:00.749 --> 00:13:06.250
The important thing to know is that you can
hit our servers and we would love you to use

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:07.250
this.

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:09.470
It helps us and helps the users.

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:15.189
We do need you to respect all the different
other people who are accessing the service.

00:13:15.189 --> 00:13:18.110
We have some usage guidelines we want you
to use.

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:24.119
We want you to limit the URL request to less
than three calls per second.

00:13:24.119 --> 00:13:30.449
If you have a large job let's say you have
a whole huge list of IDs we want you to limit

00:13:30.449 --> 00:13:39.829
them to week days, weekends, or weekday nights
Easter Standar Time, that way more and more

00:13:39.829 --> 00:13:44.199
people can access the servers without too
much traffic.

00:13:44.199 --> 00:13:50.470
We have to warn you that if you do not comply
with this policy, you could be blocked until

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:52.350
we can talk with you.

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:55.179
And try to help you resolve the issue.

00:13:55.179 --> 00:13:58.929
Most of the time, it's just that one tweak
fixes the problem.

00:13:58.929 --> 00:14:02.470
If we don't know what that is, we can help
you.

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:10.179
One thing you can currently do is send a email
to our eutilities email address letting us

00:14:10.179 --> 00:14:16.550
know what your tool and addresses are and
include that in your EUtilities call.

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:19.319
If there are problems with the script we have
an email address.

00:14:19.319 --> 00:14:22.529
We will try to contact you before we block
you.

00:14:22.529 --> 00:14:27.110
If you don't use that information or put it
in there and we don't have any idea who to

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:28.110
contact.

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:32.399
we'll try to find the closest IP address we
can to the one that sending the script and

00:14:32.399 --> 00:14:34.519
block it.

00:14:34.519 --> 00:14:44.220
Coming up, anticipated late March/April 2018,
we will be initiating a brand-new EUtilities

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:50.600
API registration system that will allow you
to actually register your own email address

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:51.680
and your API.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:57.300
We will give you an API key that you can use
in all your URL calls.

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:01.889
That will help us manage our services and
we'll give you a bonus for doing it.

00:15:01.889 --> 00:15:07.230
We will allow you to make call rates faster
than three per second.

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:09.350
Hopefully it will help you out as well.

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:14.069
We will have more and more information as
we get closer to that date about how to access

00:15:14.069 --> 00:15:18.720
it and register and what you need to do.

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:21.240
There are lots of other API tools at NCBI.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:27.470
One of the ones that many people haven't heard
about is one of the suite of PubMed Central

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:35.040
tools that have been developed to help people
who are using our PMC database.

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:40.079
There's one in particular called the ID converter
which I think might be helpful.

00:15:40.079 --> 00:15:47.240
If you are submitting a manuscript because
you have NIH funding or even those from PMC

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:53.879
Canada or you are submitting things to PMC
in Europe, you may want to take your manuscript

00:15:53.879 --> 00:15:59.920
ID and find out what the final PMC id is.

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:11.040
You can correlate PMC IDs with PM IDs or even
the digital object identifiers, the DOI.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:15.160
This works specifically for articles listed
in PMC.

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:21.910
Not everything in PubMed will work only if
they have an article in PMC.

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:23.500
Examples of things you can do.

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:31.040
Let's say you have a NIHMS ID because you
submitted it in your grant processing.

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:34.059
You can find the PMCID or the PMID for that.

00:16:34.059 --> 00:16:42.259
You can correlate PMIDs with PMCIDs that have
free full text articles and find DOIs.

00:16:42.259 --> 00:16:47.370
There is a base URL for the PMCID converter
as well.

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:49.180
You will notice it doesn't have EUtils in
it.

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:57.110
It actually has PMC as the database and idconv
because it's the converter.

00:16:57.110 --> 00:17:05.559
That's the beginning of all of the URL calls
you will use for the particular API.

00:17:05.559 --> 00:17:08.870
There is a PMC developer resources page.

00:17:08.870 --> 00:17:11.180
Here is the URL.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:12.620
It's /pmc/tools/developers.

00:17:12.620 --> 00:17:18.250
There is all sorts of different APIs they've
developed with the PubMed central group.

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:24.040
The ID converter will get you to a page that
tells you how to use the information.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:28.260
I have a number of different examples.

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:30.780
Very similar to the EUtilities calls.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:36.990
The difference is the base URL is different
and then again the parameters are slightly

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:37.990
different.

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:43.180
You can find your information back in regular
XML but other formats as well, things like

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:46.270
json or even csv.

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:50.140
I am not going to go through examples.

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:59.570
But on the ftp site, if you look for the URL,
that go.usa.gov, I actually have all of these

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:01.160
things worked out with screenshots.

00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:03.010
You can take a look.

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:08.370
If you copy paste them directly into a browser
you can see what it looks like.

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:10.040
Usage guidelines.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:17.020
The PMC crew would like you to know that you
can add a lot of identifiers in each individual

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:22.560
request, but don't mix the types of IDs the
API will not work.

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:30.220
In other words if you are starting off with
NIHMS IDs just stick a bunch of them in one

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:31.220
row.

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:35.899
Do not mix a NIHMS with a PubMed or a DOI.

00:18:35.899 --> 00:18:43.160
You can use up to 200 IDs . You could just
daisychain those together with commas.

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:48.770
If you are a larger number, split it up so
you have less than 200 per call.

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:54.100
If you have a really large set there's actually
a file in the FTP site you may want to take

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:55.390
a look at.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:19:04.560
It does a direct correlation of PMC IDs with
NIHMS with PubMed with DOI.

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:10.520
Another API at NCBI and this is our second
most popular is BLAST.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.370
They call it a BLAST URL API.

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:19.430
It will allow you to run sequence similarity
searches remotely on our servers.

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:21.320
It's a three-step process.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:24.720
You have to do all three otherwise you won't
get your results.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:29.930
You submit the job to the server and tell
them what you want them to do.

00:19:29.930 --> 00:19:34.240
Then you have to let the BLAST calculations
process.

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:40.340
You want to check the information to see if
the job is done and when it is done you can

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:43.650
retrieve the information.

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:46.920
There's lots of things you can do you can
search for an identical or near identical

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:49.150
sequence to find out more about it.

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:56.160
You can look for potential homologues you
can find where a sequence matches on the genome.

00:19:56.160 --> 00:20:00.510
You can do a lot of things with BLAST.

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:06.550
The BLAST URL API base here has BLAST in it
and then blast.cgi.

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:15.580
It will identify a blast API call as one different
from a PubMed central ID convertor.

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:20.920
There's lots of information on BLAST.

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:28.280
On the home page there's a help link and if
you click on it you can get lots of information

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:30.050
about BLAST in general.

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:35.950
On the right-hand side is Developer information
which gets you to a page that will help you

00:20:35.950 --> 00:20:41.370
use the developer tools including the BLAST
REST interfaces.

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:47.820
Clicking on one of the documentation links,
will take you over to a GitHub page.

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:56.440
That page gives you all the information about
the URL API and also see more information.

00:20:56.440 --> 00:21:01.060
All the different parameters to customize
your BLAST searches are listed on that page

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:04.200
as well.

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:10.490
Just like the PMC ID converter I will not
go through this specific example.

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:17.020
The slides are available on the FTP site which
steps through each of the processes.

00:21:17.020 --> 00:21:22.560
Submitting a query and in this particular
case we will try to find sequences that are

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:28.030
similar to the human acetylcholinesteras in
the PDB database which happens to be a structure

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:30.020
database.

00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:35.660
We will use the blastp program and again the
database is PDB.

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:41.730
The most important point I want to make is
the usage guidelines for our BLAST APIs.

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:46.760
You can submit calls and we would like you
because of the intense nature of the calculations

00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:53.320
to keep the calls three seconds apart for
the initiation of each of the searches.

00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:57.480
Probably more important than anything else
is for the second step, where you have to

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:04.100
check on the progress of your BLAST search
with the RID or request identifier.

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:09.800
You need to let the BLAST search perform its
calculations before you check.

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:17.000
Please consider a delay between step one and
step two for at least one minute to let the

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:19.830
BLAST search do its thing.

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:26.050
After you search, you are looking for a status
equals Ready to let you know the calculation

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:27.770
is done.

00:22:27.770 --> 00:22:35.650
If that is not there, you can check again
and we request you put a 30 second spacer

00:22:35.650 --> 00:22:38.290
in between each subsequent check.

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:41.370
Once the ready status exists you can get your
results.

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:46.630
There are a lot of different formats you can
get them in from text XML even to a really

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:50.080
cool tabular format.

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:53.640
Please limit your BLAST searches to less than
100 more per day.

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:59.760
If you need to do more we've created a service
called blast in the cloud which you can find

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:01.840
on the GitHub page.

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:11.090
You can use the script against the BLAST servers
in the cloud or use BLAST yourself.

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:18.460
Our third API want to hit is specifically
designed for our chemical database, PubChem,

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:25.520
and they call the PubChem API, PUG REST.

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:31.430
It's very restful and there are two main types
of PUG REST calls.

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:36.840
One of them and you can see the base URL on
right-hand side of the page they start off

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:40.120
with pubchem that's how you know they are
PubChem APIs.

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:44.830
One of them says pug_view and one is just
pug.

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:49.290
I will show you the difference in just a second.

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:55.000
Different examples you can do is you can search
for chemical synonyms and let's say you have

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:57.680
propanol you want to know what all the different
names are.

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:01.970
Like tamoxifen or estrogen.

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:07.030
You can find properties for chemicals things
like molecular weight.

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:10.440
You could get sections of PubChem compound
records.

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:14.410
Let's see you have tamoxifen and you want
to know what the pharmicology information

00:24:14.410 --> 00:24:19.650
is.

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:22.560
You can pull that down out of the record.

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:27.750
You can see the two dimensional structure
for a chemical with a single URL cal.

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:35.200
There's a lot of information and a lot of
examples in the PUG REST help documentation.

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:36.860
How to find that.

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:43.860
From the PubChem homepage under the services
tab, there's a link to PUG REST which has

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:50.440
a lot of text information . It's a very long
document, but here's the beginning of the

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:56.960
table of contents that will help you step
through the process of creating these URLs.

00:24:56.960 --> 00:24:58.770
There's a number of different URLs.

00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:08.020
Again the examples here are worked out in
the PowerPoint and PDF that is on the FTP

00:25:08.020 --> 00:25:09.020
site.

00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:17.870
The pug view URL which is listed here as pug_view/data
will allow you to pull out whole sections

00:25:17.870 --> 00:25:21.330
of the PubChem compound records.

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:28.960
Use the headings from the table of contents
to get all the synonyms or compound identifiers

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:34.560
for 3100 which happens to be diphenhydramine
or Benadryl.

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:36.680
You can also look for a different heading.

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:38.120
MeSH pharmacological classifications.

00:25:38.120 --> 00:25:44.900
This is a helpful section to talk about the
pharmacological actions that this particular

00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:47.210
chemical diphenhydramine has.

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:49.220
Notice we have JSON on here.

00:25:49.220 --> 00:25:54.390
They provide formats of json and xml and others.

00:25:54.390 --> 00:26:02.090
The caveat for the pug_view which will pull
out individual sections of the PubChem compound

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:09.420
record is it only works with the IDs or compound
identifiers in other words this number 3100.

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:11.700
How do you find the compound identifiers?

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:15.420
Let's say you have the name Benadryl and you
want to look something up.

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:20.820
You can use the EUtilities, remember the eutils
work with virtually all of our databases,

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:27.720
or you can use the other type of PUG REST
API, that's called PUB, which will allow you

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:31.110
to do more than see the compound identifiers.

00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:37.210
As an example here it says just /rest/pug
and not pug view.

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:41.750
Compound which is the name of the database,
name, and Benadryl.

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:49.180
Name is the type of input you have here, Benadryl;
you can get back the CID's in the text information.

00:26:49.180 --> 00:26:53.200
You can get lots of information including
concatenated stuff as well as pictures.

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:56.740
The usage guidelines.

00:26:56.740 --> 00:27:00.210
Please keep them less than five requests per
second.

00:27:00.210 --> 00:27:05.200
If you want large sets of information the
PubChem FTP site has a lot on there.

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:11.070
Finally I want to introduce you quickly to
an alternative.

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:16.920
For people who want the data but they don't
want to do the scripting, you can use a thing

00:27:16.920 --> 00:27:20.060
called Entrez or E-direct.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:30.800
It's not an API of itself it's a suite of
applications that use the Entrez utilities.

00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:36.751
Lots of information about how to use the E-direct
tools are not only on the bookshelf and we

00:27:36.751 --> 00:27:42.330
happen to have an E-direct cookbook that will
allow you to see examples.

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:45.300
We also have training programs webinars and
courses.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:53.470
They are hyperlinks you can get to as well
as four-part YouTube videos put out by our

00:27:53.470 --> 00:27:55.610
PubMed customer service group.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:59.620
As a matter of fact we are teaching courses
on it.

00:27:59.620 --> 00:28:05.320
Peter Cooper will be teaching a course in
E-direct on 18 September at the NIH.

00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:09.820
If you happen to be in Bethesda or the DC
Metro area.

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:14.770
The big thing about E-direct os that you use
line commands.

00:28:14.770 --> 00:28:17.460
You don't have to write scripts.

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:19.700
You can customize what you get back.

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:24.320
It's not just searching for what is in the
database and retrieving it but you are able

00:28:24.320 --> 00:28:28.590
to parse it and reformat it and download it.

00:28:28.590 --> 00:28:33.500
How to find information about E-direct it's
actually in the Entrez programming utilites

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:35.110
book that you saw before.

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:38.080
It's a whole chapter.

00:28:38.080 --> 00:28:43.310
And then the Entrez Direct cookbook on GitHub
has lots of examples.

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:50.250
We are accumulating more, in part with Ben
Busby's hackathon program.

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:55.262
Just to let you know we have a number of other
APIs, the variation services or the sequence

00:28:55.262 --> 00:28:58.730
position deletion insertion, SPDI.

00:28:58.730 --> 00:29:05.240
They will allow you to play with the different
variation servers we have here at NCBI.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:12.840
The genome remapping service is similar to
the Entrez Direct in that it's a Perl script

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:20.180
to download that allows you to help coordinate
mapping on one genomic assembly to another.

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:23.330
That is a lot of information.

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:25.700
How do I keep up with it?

00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:29.680
From our NCBI homesite we have a thing called
Develop.

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:34.330
This will take you to a developers site to
give you more information than you probably

00:29:34.330 --> 00:29:39.100
ever need about our tools including our APIs
listed there.

00:29:39.100 --> 00:29:41.400
That gives you a whole page.

00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:48.990
We will be updating this set of APIs very
shortly to add more and more.

00:29:48.990 --> 00:29:50.240
I am wrapping up.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:55.370
As you may imagine we have lots of resources
here at the NCBI.

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:58.100
Also at the national Library of medicine.

00:29:58.100 --> 00:30:01.310
Where they have additional APIs you might
be interested in.

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:05.940
Here's the URL where you can find out more
about all the APIs that the national Library

00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:08.610
of medicine provides.

00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:15.990
We will be cataloguing not just these APIs
but even more that we have and we will be

00:30:15.990 --> 00:30:20.600
putting them on that Develop page that you
just saw.

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:26.420
There are webinars like this and courses like
peters to help.

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:33.440
You can get those on the NCBI Learn site for
more information.

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:39.720
We want to introduce you to the concept of
hackathons, where scripters may use APIs to

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:40.720
create tools.

00:30:40.720 --> 00:30:47.140
Ben Busby is coordinating one in Pittsburg
at the end of September that will enlist use

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:51.850
of APIs in order to help create really cool
tools.

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:54.020
There's lots of information I covered.

00:30:54.020 --> 00:31:00.789
These are the base URLs for all the documentation
for each individual API I talked about.

00:31:00.789 --> 00:31:07.340
We have a NCBI insights blog were a lot of
things are posted about APIs and other services

00:31:07.340 --> 00:31:10.360
at NCBI and of course the YouTube website.

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:16.720
We have an email address for help on all the
resources including the APIs that you may

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:19.980
be using and the FTP site for lots of data.

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:27.700
We encourage you to send messages in, so we
can help you learn to use the APIs effectively.

00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:30.960
Thank you very much for listening.

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:33.640
All the information will be on the FTP site.

00:31:33.640 --> 00:31:39.110
We look forward to hearing from you on the
survey as well to help guide us in providing

00:31:39.110 --> 00:31:43.000
more and better information about what we
have at NCBI.

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:44.370
Thank you.

